From ce6b8b2e9ab9f0e27740ff0970ba305f0223f76e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@lccc.local>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:15:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] x86-64: fuse compares through flag-neutral widening moves
 (IS-07)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The clamp idiom `x > 100 ? 100 : x` lowered as
cmp/select/select-with-widened-arm lost its flag fusion: the select's
arm value needed an I32->I64 widening (`movslq %edi, %rdx`) BETWEEN the
cmp and the select, and the fusion chain walk treated any intervening
instruction as a chain breaker — falling back to
setcc+movzbl+testq+cmovne (4 extra instructions per clamp).

The chain walk now steps over widening integer casts of UNRELATED
values: their emission is pure data movement on every path (mature
direct move, accumulator staging, MachInst-queued movslq) and leaves
EFLAGS untouched, so the compare's flags are still live at the select.
The Cmp destination's single-use invariant is checked before the walk,
so the boolean is still consumed by exactly the same select.

Deliberately NOT accepted as transparent: UnaryOp (Neg/Not write
flags), BinOp (writes flags), GEP (scaled indices may emit imul), and
anything touching memory or the pending boolean — those break the walk
exactly as before.

`limit` now emits cmp/movslq/movq/cmovg (the setcc chain is gone);
measured on the clamp trio with GCC as reference. Validated: lib gate
1302/0 under -D warnings; x86-64 corpus 962/0; i686 failure set
unchanged; godbolt x86-64 census unchanged. The remaining known gap —
nested selects whose producer cmps interleave
(cmp9, cmp14, select(cond=14), select(cond=9): the outer select's
producer flags are clobbered by the inner cmp) — needs emission
reordering against flag liveness and is recorded for a follow-up.
