From 94e002cbd9182c3717b376d97cdfde15c28288fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@lccc.local>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:15:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] x86-64: materialize non-negative immediates with movl
 (PF-16)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`movq $imm32, %r64` assembles to REX.W C7 /0 (7 bytes). For immediate
values >= 0, `movl $imm, %r32` (B8+r, 5 bytes, no REX) writes the
identical canonical 64-bit value — a 32-bit destination write
zero-extends, which equals the q-form's sign extension exactly when the
immediate's high bit is clear. Negative immediates keep the q-form
(movl would zero-extend the wrong value), and magnitudes beyond
imm32 keep movabsq. The register-direct constant-materialization sites
(I8/I16/I32/I64 consts into a target register, both the PhysReg and
register-name variants) and the two flag-neutral `movq $0` clears in
the compare emitter now pick the shortest correct form; the $0 sites
additionally drop to movl (same zero-canonical value, neither form
touches flags, two bytes shorter).

Instruction counts are unchanged by construction (one mov either way),
so the oracle census is stable; the win is 2 bytes + a dropped REX
prefix per immediate in switch jump-table staging, GEP offsets, and
compare-materialization paths.

Validated: value-checked kernels (positive/negative/beyond-imm32
